      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2615.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 711,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Physical States of Water\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The fluid we drink, swim in, and use to wash.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and hail.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, clouds, and steam from boiling water.\n\n### 2. Unique and Miraculous Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a transport system, carrying chemicals, minerals, and nutrients wherever it goes (through our bloodstreams, through plant stems, and through the earth).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze solid from the bottom up, destroying all aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This regulates the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which is how water climbs up the roots of a tall tree against gravity).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite Earth looking wet from space, **96.5% of all Earth's water is salt water** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is fresh water**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and ponds.\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nWater is the essential ingredient for life as we know it. \n*   **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, and our lungs are about 83%. Without water, human cells cannot maintain their structure, chemical reactions cannot happen, and we would die within a few days.\n*   **Evolution:** Life began in the oceans billions of years ago, and every living thing—from the smallest bacteria to the largest blue whale—depends on it.\n\n**In short:** Water is the lifeblood of planet Earth. It shapes our geography, controls our weather, and makes up the majority of our own bodies."
